1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,812
A pipeline can run when you ask it to, or on a schedule.

2
00:00:04,812 --> 00:00:09,361
In this video, we schedule a pipeline with a workflow, and then read

3
00:00:09,361 --> 00:00:13,910
the run history to see what each run did and why one of them failed.

4
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:23,124
A run executes the published version of a pipeline, never its draft.

5
00:00:23,124 --> 00:00:27,800
It is queued and executes in the background, so no page waits for it.

6
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:33,796
A run reads and writes data with the access of the user who published the pipeline.

7
00:00:33,796 --> 00:00:37,745
Only one run of a pipeline executes at a time, and the others

8
00:00:37,745 --> 00:00:39,160
wait in the queue.

9
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:42,574
A pipeline has no schedule of its own.

10
00:00:42,574 --> 00:00:44,390
A workflow provides one:

11
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:49,536
a Timer Event starts the workflow, and a Run Data Pipeline Task queues the run.

12
00:00:49,536 --> 00:00:54,500
These activities need the Workflows and Timer Workflows Activities features.

13
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:04,869
Under Tools, Data Pipelines, Pipelines, each pipeline shows its published version and the

14
00:01:04,869 --> 00:01:06,721
status of its last run.

15
00:01:06,721 --> 00:01:11,640
A pipeline that was never published, or that is disabled, can't be run.

16
00:01:12,181 --> 00:01:17,208
From the Actions menu of Blog posts export, we start a run, and confirm with Run.

17
00:01:17,208 --> 00:01:21,831
The same menu has Run history, which lists the runs of this pipeline only.

18
00:01:22,643 --> 00:01:25,973
The run is queued and executes in the background.

19
00:01:25,973 --> 00:01:30,758
Its page shows the status, here Succeeded, the version that ran, and when

20
00:01:30,758 --> 00:01:33,423
the run was queued, started and ended.

21
00:01:37,651 --> 00:01:39,094
Let's schedule it.

22
00:01:39,094 --> 00:01:44,745
Under Design, Workflows, we choose Create Workflow, name the workflow Nightly blog posts

23
00:01:44,745 --> 00:01:47,991
export, and choose Create and open the designer.

24
00:01:48,449 --> 00:01:53,144
A workflow starts with an event, and the designer offers the common ones.

25
00:01:53,144 --> 00:01:58,789
We choose Timer Event, which runs the workflow repeatedly according to a CRON expression.

26
00:01:59,267 --> 00:02:05,047
In the CRON Expression field, we enter a schedule for two in the morning, every day.

27
00:02:05,047 --> 00:02:09,927
A CRON expression uses UTC by default, so we check Use site time zone.

28
00:02:10,388 --> 00:02:14,201
Pointing at the Done outcome of the Timer Event shows a plus.

29
00:02:14,201 --> 00:02:18,070
We click it, search for Run Data Pipeline Task, and choose it.

30
00:02:18,070 --> 00:02:20,688
The designer adds it, already connected.

31
00:02:21,138 --> 00:02:24,068
In Data pipeline, we choose Blog posts export.

32
00:02:24,068 --> 00:02:27,899
We also check Skip when a run is already queued or running, so

33
00:02:27,899 --> 00:02:30,378
that runs do not pile up in the queue.

34
00:02:30,844 --> 00:02:34,373
Publish turns the draft into the published workflow.

35
00:02:34,373 --> 00:02:38,693
From then on, the workflow queues a run every night, and each run

36
00:02:38,693 --> 00:02:40,944
is recorded in the run history.

37
00:02:45,162 --> 00:02:50,608
Under Tools, Data Pipelines, Run History, the runs of every pipeline are listed,

38
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:52,381
the most recent first.

39
00:02:52,381 --> 00:02:57,066
Each run shows its status, the version that ran, when it was queued,

40
00:02:57,066 --> 00:02:59,852
how long it took, and what started it.

41
00:03:00,307 --> 00:03:04,155
The last badge, with the play icon, says what started the run:

42
00:03:04,155 --> 00:03:08,285
Manual, followed by a user name, for a run started from the Actions

43
00:03:08,285 --> 00:03:11,567
menu, and Workflow for a run that a workflow queued.

44
00:03:12,024 --> 00:03:16,360
Search by pipeline narrows the list by pipeline name, and the Status menu

45
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:17,841
narrows it by status.

46
00:03:17,841 --> 00:03:19,104
We choose Failed.

47
00:03:20,713 --> 00:03:23,191
We open the failed run with Details.

48
00:03:23,191 --> 00:03:27,498
Its page shows the status and the version, when the run was queued,

49
00:03:27,498 --> 00:03:30,743
started and ended, and the error that stopped it.

50
00:03:31,194 --> 00:03:37,394
The Steps table lists each step with its status, rows read and written, files, and warnings.

51
00:03:37,394 --> 00:03:42,265
This run stopped before its first step began, so every step is Pending.

52
00:03:42,265 --> 00:03:46,504
The Log repeats the error in red, after the name of the step.

53
00:03:46,965 --> 00:03:50,731
On the page of a run that succeeded, the Steps table shows the

54
00:03:50,731 --> 00:03:54,995
rows and files of each step, and Delivered lists what the run produced,

55
00:03:54,995 --> 00:03:58,705
such as a file saved to the media library, with a link to it.

56
00:04:02,905 --> 00:04:06,288
In the Actions menu of a run, Cancel the run stops it at

57
00:04:06,288 --> 00:04:09,888
once if it is queued, or at its next batch if it is running.

58
00:04:09,888 --> 00:04:12,561
It needs the Run data pipelines permission.

59
00:04:12,561 --> 00:04:15,725
By default, runs are deleted 30 days after they end.

60
00:04:17,725 --> 00:04:22,721
A schedule is one workflow away, and every run leaves a record you can read.

61
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:24,305
Thanks for watching.

